<p>If you need TWIA claim help in Corpus Christi, the first thing to know is that a Texas Windstorm Insurance Association claim does not follow the same rules as an ordinary homeowners claim. TWIA claims are governed by Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, which sets specific deadlines for filing, for TWIA's decision, for payment, and for disputes. Missing one of those deadlines can affect your rights. This page walks through how a TWIA claim works for property owners in Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and the surrounding Coastal Bend, and how a licensed public adjuster may help.</p>

<h2>Who Has TWIA Coverage on the Coastal Bend</h2>
<p>TWIA was created by the Texas Legislature in 1971 as the wind and hail insurer for the Texas coast. Its designated catastrophe area includes 14 first-tier coastal counties, among them Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg (<a href="https://www.twia.org/about-us/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TWIA overview</a>). Many private insurers exclude wind and hail for properties in these counties, so homes and businesses from Calallen and Flour Bluff to Portland, Rockport, Port Aransas, and Kingsville often carry a TWIA policy alongside a homeowners policy and a flood policy.</p>
<p>TWIA policies cover wind and hail only. They do not cover flood or storm surge. That split is the source of many disputes after a hurricane; see our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page and the <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge guide</a>.</p>

<h2>TWIA Claim Deadlines Under Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1</h2>
<p>The table below summarizes the main deadlines in the statute (<a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210</a>). It is a plain-English summary, not legal advice. Deadlines can be extended in some situations, and the exact start date for each clock matters. For questions about how a deadline applies to your claim, or about filing suit, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Section</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>File the claim with TWIA</td><td>No later than one year after the date the damage occurs; the Commissioner may extend by up to 180 days for good cause</td><td>&sect;2210.573(a), &sect;2210.205</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA asks for missing information</td><td>Within 30 days after the claim is filed</td><td>&sect;2210.573(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA accepts, partly accepts, or denies</td><td>Written notice within 60 days after receiving the claim or the requested information, whichever is later (the Commissioner may extend)</td><td>&sect;2210.573(d), &sect;2210.581</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA pays the accepted amount</td><td>Within 10 days after the acceptance notice (or after a required act by the claimant)</td><td>&sect;2210.5731</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demand appraisal over the amount</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the acceptance or partial-acceptance notice; 30 more days possible on a written, good-cause request made within 15 days after the 60 days end</td><td>&sect;2210.574</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contest a denial: notice of intent to sue</td><td>Before the limitations period expires; TWIA may request mediation or a moderated settlement conference within 60 days of the notice, to be completed within 60 days of the request</td><td>&sect;2210.575</td></tr>
<tr><td>File suit over a denial</td><td>No later than two years after receiving the partial or full denial notice (a statute of repose)</td><td>&sect;2210.577</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Two more points are worth knowing. First, the general Chapter 542A pre-suit notice process does not apply to actions against TWIA (&sect;542A.002(b)); TWIA disputes follow Subchapter L-1 instead. Second, the Texas Department of Insurance runs an ombudsman program to help TWIA policyholders with the claims process (&sect;2210.582). Our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> can help you keep track of dates, but it does not replace reading your notices carefully.</p>

