<h2>Documenting a TWIA Wind or Hail Claim</h2>
<p>Because TWIA covers wind and hail and nothing else, the documentation has to show that wind or hail caused the damage. That is straightforward for a roof with missing shingles after a hailstorm. It gets harder when the same structure also took surge, when damage could be blamed on age or wear, or when interior water damage might have come from rain through a wind-created opening or from rising water.</p>
<p>A well-supported TWIA claim usually includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Date-stamped photos and video of the roof, exterior, and interior before cleanup.</li>
<li>Evidence of the storm, such as NWS and NOAA records; our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> tool is a starting point.</li>
<li>A roof inspection showing wind creasing, lifted tabs, missing materials, or hail impacts.</li>
<li>A detailed, line-item repair estimate covering roofing, exterior, and interior work.</li>
<li>Your policy, declarations page, and any windstorm certificates for the property.</li>
<li>Receipts for tarping, board-up, and water removal.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the full <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> for more.</p>

<h2>Windstorm Certificates and Repairs</h2>
<p>Structures built, altered, remodeled, enlarged, or repaired on or after January 1, 1988 generally need a certificate of compliance to be eligible for TWIA coverage. The TDI Windstorm Inspection Program covers re-roofs and roof repairs in the catastrophe area, and inspections are done by appointed Texas-licensed engineers or TDI inspectors (<a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/generalquestio.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI windstorm program</a>). In Corpus Christi, a re-roofing permit application includes a completed TDI WPI-1 form. Building repairs into the claim with certification in mind can help avoid problems with future coverage. Our <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">WPI-8 guide</a> covers the details.</p>

<h2>What to Do in the First Days After Wind or Hail Damage</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Report the claim to TWIA</strong> as soon as you can, and write down the claim number and the date you filed.</li>
<li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Photograph the roof, every side of the building, and each damaged room.</li>
<li><strong>Make temporary repairs</strong> such as tarping or boarding up to prevent more damage, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Report other policies too.</strong> If surge or rising water also reached the property, notify your flood insurer separately.</li>
<li><strong>Read every letter from TWIA</strong> and note the date you received it, because several deadlines run from the date of a notice.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log</strong> of calls, emails, inspections, and payments.</li>
</ol>
<p>If TWIA has already paid and the amount looks low, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can compare TWIA's scope against the actual damage before the appraisal window closes. Our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page explains how appraisal generally works.</p>

<h2>Common Disputes on TWIA Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind vs. water.</strong> TWIA may attribute damage to flood or surge, which its policy does not cover.</li>
<li><strong>Wear and tear.</strong> Granule loss, aging shingles, or corrosion from salt air may be blamed instead of the storm.</li>
<li><strong>Scope and pricing.</strong> The estimate may leave out items, allow repairs where replacement is needed, or use pricing that does not match the local market.</li>
<li><strong>Deductibles.</strong> Wind/hail deductibles are often a percentage of the dwelling limit; check your declarations page and see our <a href="/named-storm-hurricane-deductibles-texas/">deductible guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>TWIA Claim Help From a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the policyholder only. On a TWIA claim, that may mean reviewing the policy and certificates, inspecting the property, preparing a detailed estimate, assembling weather evidence, responding to TWIA's information requests on time, and tracking each statutory deadline. If TWIA accepts the claim but you disagree with the amount, we can help you evaluate whether appraisal makes sense within the 60-day window. If TWIA denies coverage, we will tell you plainly that the next steps are legal ones and that you should consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<p>No one can promise how TWIA will decide a claim. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
